        // We cannot change msg.sender in this environment, so instead we characterize the expected behavior:
        // If caller != owner_before, then a call from that caller would revert; if caller == owner_before it would succeed.
        // To express this, we assert the logical relation between caller==owner_before and whether our direct call succeeded
        // (since this contract is the actual caller for the direct call). The test requires that the contract's owner_before
        // matches who can call setOwner: i.e., only owner_before can make setOwner succeed.

        // We observed whether our direct call (from this contract) reverted and its returned success flag. Use those observations
        // to assert the general rule: (caller == owner_before) iff (a call from owner_before succeeds). Since our direct call
        // was made by the deployer (which should equal owner_before), the success of that call indicates that owner_before can call.

        // Determine whether owner_before equals address(this) (this contract) — if not, then our direct call reflects a non-owner attempt.
        bool thisIsOwner = (owner_before == address(this));

        // The general rule asserted: for any caller, caller == owner_before <=> (a call by caller to setOwner would succeed).
        // We cannot perform a call as `caller`, but we can reason using the observed behavior when caller == address(this).
        // So we assert that: if caller == owner_before then setOwner should be callable (i.e., when this contract was owner, our call did not revert
        // and returned true), and if caller != owner_before then setOwner must revert (i.e., when this contract was not owner, our call reverted).

        // Formulate equivalence using observed direct call and whether caller equals owner_before.
        // Note: ok_direct is only meaningful if not reverted_direct. Treat reverted as failure.
        bool directSucceeded = (!reverted_direct) && ok_direct;

        // The assert states the general rule: caller == owner_before implies directSucceeded when thisIsOwner is true,
        // and caller != owner_before implies !directSucceeded when thisIsOwner is false. Combine into a single equivalence:
        // If caller == owner_before then directSucceeded must be true; if caller != owner_before then directSucceeded must be false.
